WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:02.350
MATT PARKER: It is with great pleasure that
I bring to the stage,

00:00:02.350 --> 00:00:07.700
both a friend of mine, and famously "that
guy from the internet":

00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:10.180
he's wearing a slightly off-red shirt,

00:00:10.180 --> 00:00:13.600
can you please put your hands together and
welcome to the stage, it's Tom Scott!

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:16.600
TOM SCOTT: Thank you!

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:19.800
Where's the clicker? Matt, where'd you put
the clicker?

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:25.320
Ah! Great. Black clicker, black table. It's
wonderful.

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:27.020
Hello, my name is Tom Scott. Hello!

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:31.339
Wonderful, you're alive. I am an accidental
emoji expert.

00:00:31.339 --> 00:00:34.649
I didn't mean -- also, sorry, let's get one
thing out of the way,

00:00:34.649 --> 00:00:37.269
this is going to be less of a polished talk
than I normally do,

00:00:37.269 --> 00:00:38.709
there are two reasons for that:

00:00:38.709 --> 00:00:39.989
one, I didn't quite rehearse as much as I
should,

00:00:39.989 --> 00:00:42.400
and two, I hate shtick.

00:00:42.410 --> 00:00:47.129
I hate the idea that you become "that person
known for that thing"

00:00:47.129 --> 00:00:49.739
and yet I've plenty of shticks in my time.

00:00:49.739 --> 00:00:51.819
I had the pirate shtick, that was a bad year.

00:00:53.060 --> 00:00:56.160
And now I have the emoji shtick. And I didn't
mean to.

00:00:56.160 --> 00:00:58.720
I didn't mean to. But me and a friend came
up with an idea,

00:00:58.730 --> 00:01:03.010
we built a messenger on your phone that you
can only send emoji through.

00:01:03.010 --> 00:01:05.930
Even your username had to be emoji.

00:01:05.930 --> 00:01:09.680
And we get 70,000 people signing up to that
because they all went

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:12.750
"What? I've got to get an emoji as my username?
What do I do?"

00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:15.860
and it turns out you can be rude in emoji,
really creatively.

00:01:15.860 --> 00:01:18.520
We thought we wouldn't have to put a swear
filter on that.

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:22.300
We did. Lot of aubergines. A lot of aubergines.

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:26.980
We killed that, after just under a year.

00:01:26.980 --> 00:01:29.380
But then I went and make this, which is a
full size emoji keyboard.

00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:34.079
I say full size, it's 14 keyboards. There
are a lot of emoji. All hooked up.

00:01:34.079 --> 00:01:38.090
But as a result of that, it turns out that
I now know

00:01:38.090 --> 00:01:41.540
most of the technical details of how emoji
works.

00:01:41.540 --> 00:01:45.969
Also, it turns out that I am now an expert
on emoji to the extent

00:01:45.969 --> 00:01:48.380
that Radio 2 called me up and said 'can you
bring your keyboard in',

00:01:48.380 --> 00:01:50.040
I thought: Radio. Keyboard.

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:54.130
And literally, there was a moment where they
said

00:01:54.130 --> 00:01:55.940
"could you just... what does it sound like
to type on?"

00:01:55.940 --> 00:01:57.880
Well, it sounds like a keyboard.

00:01:57.880 --> 00:02:03.200
So I did that. So I am an accidental and grumpy
emoji expert.

00:02:03.210 --> 00:02:05.490
These are the first emoji.

00:02:05.490 --> 00:02:07.759
These are the first ones that were ever made.

00:02:07.759 --> 00:02:10.119
I think they've been coloured here, which
they weren't normally,

00:02:10.119 --> 00:02:13.870
but one engineer in Japan, decided that they
had a bit --

00:02:13.870 --> 00:02:18.130
because computers, which is all the detail
you're getting in a 10-minute talk,

00:02:19.220 --> 00:02:20.800
there was a bit of room left over.

00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:24.140
So there's the idea of text encoding, which
is where you take

00:02:24.150 --> 00:02:26.410
the letters than we write in, and you turn
them into 1s and 0s

00:02:26.410 --> 00:02:28.319
that computers can understand.

00:02:28.319 --> 00:02:32.810
America kind of did that first? Or at least,
did the big worldwide version of that.

00:02:32.810 --> 00:02:36.610
So that's great, you have A-Z, 0-9, doesn't
take up that much room.

00:02:36.610 --> 00:02:38.650
Japan: loads more characters, you needed a
bit more room.

00:02:38.650 --> 00:02:41.379
And because computers, they had a bit of space
left over.

00:02:41.379 --> 00:02:44.319
So one engineer goes, you know what? We can
put pictures in there.

00:02:44.319 --> 00:02:47.560
'Cos we're basically sending a load of different
pictures for each character

00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:50.489
let's add some more. So those are the first
emoji.

00:02:50.489 --> 00:02:52.620
Some transport, some star signs, some numbers.

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:55.090
And they're just used on one mobile network.

00:02:55.090 --> 00:02:58.530
And then the next mobile network went "that
is a brilliant idea,

00:02:58.530 --> 00:03:02.209
"we're having that." Only they used a completely
different and incompatible system

00:03:02.209 --> 00:03:05.920
and then a third network did the same. And
this was fine.

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:07.849
They didn't work together.

00:03:07.849 --> 00:03:12.240
And then, along came the Unicode Consortium!

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:14.680
Which does sound like a sci-fi baddie, but
they're actually

00:03:14.680 --> 00:03:17.870
the linguists and computer scientists in charge
of making sure

00:03:17.870 --> 00:03:19.920
that everyone can talk to each other online.

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:23.000
The reason that someone in Japan can send
you a text, in Japanese,

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:25.819
and it will now arrive on your phone intact
is because of these guys.

00:03:25.819 --> 00:03:30.359
The most amazing thing in computer standardisation
that's ever been done.

00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:32.320
They went to France, they looked at France,

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:34.060
they said "you're mostly like English, that's
fine,

00:03:34.060 --> 00:03:36.019
"you've got a little bit of a weird letter
here, that's fine,

00:03:36.019 --> 00:03:38.409
"we can take -- thanks, we're going to take
your letters,

00:03:38.409 --> 00:03:41.529
"and put them just there in the standard.
You OK? You're cool."

00:03:41.529 --> 00:03:43.869
All right. They went to Russia. Russia and
then all there. They said,

00:03:43.869 --> 00:03:45.629
"oh, you've got a whole different alphabet.

00:03:45.629 --> 00:03:47.309
"All right, that's fine. We're going to take
your alphabet.

00:03:47.309 --> 00:03:49.150
"Oh! Sorry, Belarus, you've got some more
letters.

00:03:49.150 --> 00:03:50.910
"Okay, we're going to take all those,

00:03:50.910 --> 00:03:52.450
"and we're going to put them just there."

00:03:52.450 --> 00:03:54.650
And then they got to Japan, and they went
"bloody hell...

00:03:56.580 --> 00:03:58.900
"right, Japan, okay, that's fine, we've got
all these,

00:03:58.900 --> 00:04:01.180
"and we'll put them right there... okay, right,
that's fine,

00:04:01.180 --> 00:04:02.900
"we're going to put them in the standard just
in...

00:04:03.400 --> 00:04:05.400
"Japan, what are those?"

00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:14.340
And Japan's like, "they're emoji. We use them
to talk."

00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:17.660
"Those are not letters."

00:04:17.660 --> 00:04:20.640
"Don't care. We use them to communicate. They're
characters.

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:23.090
"You've got to include them. You know, two-way...?"

00:04:24.860 --> 00:04:26.280
"What do you think?"

00:04:26.290 --> 00:04:29.910
"Well, we let America have the smiley face,
didn't we?"

00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:34.500
"Right, fine, fine, Japan, we're going to
take those,

00:04:34.500 --> 00:04:35.340
"we're going to put them in there,

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:36.780
"and no-one is going to care."

00:04:36.790 --> 00:04:38.870
And no-one did care, and then Apple came along.

00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:41.990
And Apple wanted to sell iPhones in Japan,

00:04:41.990 --> 00:04:43.460
so they put an emoji keyboard on there

00:04:43.460 --> 00:04:44.590
and no-one noticed for a little while

00:04:44.590 --> 00:04:48.790
and then one person in Britain -- sorry, in
America, went..

00:04:48.790 --> 00:04:51.550
"hurr! I can send piles of poo to my friends!"

00:04:53.550 --> 00:04:55.950
So they did. And their friends went "how did
you...

00:04:55.950 --> 00:04:57.680
"how did you do that? Show me!"

00:04:57.680 --> 00:05:01.470
And soon there's this ready-made viral explosion
of piles of poo.

00:05:02.360 --> 00:05:04.340
Which I guess is dysentry.

00:05:06.740 --> 00:05:09.140
And now we have emoji cushions and emoji pillows.

00:05:09.150 --> 00:05:13.800
That's a photo by my friend Dan, who was walking
around Dalston,

00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:17.130
found emoji pillows, and then found another
shop selling...

00:05:17.130 --> 00:05:22.870
turns out there's a lot of emoji pillows in
Dalston, if anyone wants one.

00:05:22.870 --> 00:05:24.860
This is the Unicode Consortium's motto.

00:05:24.860 --> 00:05:27.500
It is: "enabling people around the world
to use computers in any language".

00:05:27.500 --> 00:05:30.120
This is how the world sees the Emoji Consor--
er, the Uni--

00:05:30.120 --> 00:05:32.870
'Emoji Consortium'! Spot the Freudian slip.

00:05:32.870 --> 00:05:36.350
This is how the world sees the Unicode Consortium's
motto:

00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:43.420
So, Unicode 9's out soon. And this is how
I see them talking to the world,

00:05:43.430 --> 00:05:47.180
it's like "hey, world, Unicode 9 is out now,
and it's amazing!

00:05:47.180 --> 00:05:49.620
"It's got to Osage alphabet in it!" This is
an alphabet

00:05:49.620 --> 00:05:52.400
that was only designed a few years ago. This
is amazing.

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:56.440
"It's got the Georgian Lari symbol." Brand
new currency symbol. It's in there.

00:05:56.440 --> 00:06:00.550
They're just sat down in committe meetings,
and had really vicious fights

00:06:00.550 --> 00:06:02.820
between different representatives from different
countries

00:06:02.820 --> 00:06:05.320
about how particular languages should go in,

00:06:05.320 --> 00:06:06.820
because it's actually really complicated.

00:06:06.820 --> 00:06:09.160
And the world went "k".

00:06:11.800 --> 00:06:14.740
"By the way," they say, "we've got some new
emoji too."

00:06:14.740 --> 00:06:16.120
And suddenly we care!

00:06:24.100 --> 00:06:25.640
Does anyone know what this emoji is?

00:06:25.660 --> 00:06:27.780
Does anyone know what this emoji is, by the
way?

00:06:27.780 --> 00:06:31.960
["You!"] Is that me? Yeah, it's close. Seriously,
quick shout out...

00:06:31.970 --> 00:06:34.750
["Triumph!"] Triumph! Yes! Who was up there
first?

00:06:34.750 --> 00:06:37.630
There is a measuring tape going to the lady...

00:06:37.630 --> 00:06:39.370
all right, so this is... this is worrying.
This might not work,

00:06:39.370 --> 00:06:41.400
let's see if we can get... hey-up!

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:44.540
Missed by a mile! I did not do well at sports.

00:06:46.080 --> 00:06:49.060
Yeah, it's 'look of triumph' which no-one
actually uses it for,

00:06:49.060 --> 00:06:53.120
because it was designed in Japan, that's a
different cultural reference,

00:06:53.120 --> 00:06:54.760
which is great, so.

00:06:54.760 --> 00:06:57.170
The 2016 emoji are out soon.

00:06:57.170 --> 00:07:00.180
Here are some of them. This is from Emojipedia,
which is a thing.

00:07:00.180 --> 00:07:03.200
They allow re-use of this with credit.

00:07:03.200 --> 00:07:07.420
These are some of what we've got. This is
about the half the candidates.

00:07:07.420 --> 00:07:11.780
We've got 'nauseated face', let's be honest,
that's overdue.

00:07:11.780 --> 00:07:13.250
We've got 'duck', obviously that's vital.

00:07:14.480 --> 00:07:16.480
We don't have 'duckface', which feels like
it should be in there.

00:07:16.480 --> 00:07:18.380
We do have 'selfie'.

00:07:18.380 --> 00:07:20.740
So you can put that next to any emoji now,
just put that on it.

00:07:20.740 --> 00:07:23.380
We also have 'dancing man', and I'll get back
to him in a minute.

00:07:23.380 --> 00:07:26.640
They're going to get ratified probably in
a meeting in May 2016

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:28.190
they'll be on your phone by the end of the
year.

00:07:28.580 --> 00:07:29.480
Probably.

00:07:30.360 --> 00:07:31.460
Maybe.

00:07:31.940 --> 00:07:33.980
But what's interesting, for me at least, is
the 2017 emoji.

00:07:33.980 --> 00:07:36.940
'Cos we've had the first few candidates for
that now,

00:07:36.940 --> 00:07:40.720
we might have 'chinese takeout box' and 'skeptical
face with one eyebrow raised',

00:07:40.720 --> 00:07:42.180
but that's not the interesting thing.

00:07:42.180 --> 00:07:44.650
Because recently we've had these. We've had
skin tone modifiers.

00:07:44.650 --> 00:07:47.250
Now these are really important, these are
fantastic,

00:07:47.250 --> 00:07:49.520
because it means it's not all default yellow.

00:07:49.520 --> 00:07:53.110
As anyone who's watched the Simpsons knows,
yellow is not default neutral.

00:07:53.110 --> 00:07:56.620
But: if you want to actually make one of these,

00:07:56.620 --> 00:07:59.660
behind the scenes you are putting the regular
emoji in,

00:07:59.660 --> 00:08:03.200
and then you are putting what is called a
'skin tone modifier' or a 'tag character'.

00:08:03.200 --> 00:08:08.870
From 1 to 5 on the Fitzpatrick scale, which
is a skin tone scale.

00:08:08.870 --> 00:08:10.810
So there's actually two separate characters
there

00:08:10.810 --> 00:08:12.740
and they get merged together.

00:08:12.740 --> 00:08:16.560
The 2017 emojis are going to get more tags.
If proposal--

00:08:16.560 --> 00:08:18.710
I said this was a lot of detail--

00:08:18.710 --> 00:08:23.490
if proposal TR52 gets ratified next year.

00:08:23.490 --> 00:08:28.450
And the tag characters that are going to get
added are for gender...

00:08:28.450 --> 00:08:30.740
gender, hair colour and possibly direction.

00:08:30.740 --> 00:08:34.530
Direction's easy, it's so that everyone agrees
which way the gun is pointing

00:08:34.530 --> 00:08:37.240
when you do an emoji that someone shot someone.

00:08:37.240 --> 00:08:39.940
It's good for, like, I don't know, if you've
shot someone.

00:08:43.360 --> 00:08:47.840
Hair colour. It's going to be black, brown,
red, grey, blond and...

00:08:47.840 --> 00:08:49.160
what have I forgotten? Who have I missed?

00:08:49.160 --> 00:08:50.540
["White!"] No...

00:08:50.540 --> 00:08:54.220
Bald! That's it! No hair at all. I know at
least three people in here

00:08:54.220 --> 00:08:55.440
who have different hair colours to that.

00:08:55.440 --> 00:08:56.960
If you have an issue with that,

00:08:56.960 --> 00:08:59.560
please take it up with the Unicode Consortium,
not me.

00:09:00.400 --> 00:09:03.840
Which is taken, I swear, from the list of
hair colours that are valid for

00:09:03.850 --> 00:09:08.710
US drivers' licenses and the UN Grounds Pass.

00:09:08.710 --> 00:09:12.210
I looked at the specification, that's where
they got the list from.

00:09:12.210 --> 00:09:14.810
So you'll be able to have a different skin
colour, different hair colour,

00:09:14.810 --> 00:09:18.580
and crucially, different gender presentation.
Because:

00:09:18.590 --> 00:09:21.500
on the left is how Apple says 'dancer'.

00:09:21.500 --> 00:09:25.490
And on the right is how Android said 'dancer'
until recently.

00:09:25.490 --> 00:09:28.830
So if you were a woman and you said "I'm feeling
like this today,"

00:09:28.830 --> 00:09:33.820
it did not go well when it came through to
an Android user.

00:09:33.820 --> 00:09:36.710
So there is now a separate woman dancer and
man dancer,

00:09:36.710 --> 00:09:40.420
but for everything else the idea is you have
a character

00:09:40.420 --> 00:09:42.990
and then a gender symbol, which is great,

00:09:42.990 --> 00:09:45.050
because currently the Unicode construction
worker is male

00:09:45.050 --> 00:09:47.890
and the Unicode receptionist is female

00:09:47.890 --> 00:09:50.360
because apparently it's the 1980s.

00:09:51.340 --> 00:09:54.250
And if you think this is ridiculous, this
is my final point,

00:09:54.250 --> 00:09:56.370
if you think this is ridiculous, this is too
much,

00:09:56.370 --> 00:09:58.080
this is something no-one should care about,

00:09:58.080 --> 00:10:02.710
this is just a lot of, of... wrangling over
a minor detail,

00:10:02.710 --> 00:10:05.300
yes it sort of is. And that boardroom meeting

00:10:05.300 --> 00:10:08.710
will have a lot of things to decide about it.

00:10:08.710 --> 00:10:10.670
But if you think this is not something you
care about,

00:10:10.670 --> 00:10:13.640
just imagine how the people in that board
meeting feel.

00:10:13.640 --> 00:10:15.050
Because they've got a long list --

00:10:15.050 --> 00:10:17.300
I've seen the agenda for the last meeting,
I read it,

00:10:17.300 --> 00:10:18.890
it's got all sorts of really wonderful things.

00:10:18.890 --> 00:10:20.340
Some of the best linguistics,

00:10:20.340 --> 00:10:21.520
some of the best computer scientists,

00:10:21.520 --> 00:10:23.520
some of the best computational linguists

00:10:23.520 --> 00:10:27.260
are going to be in that room deciding really
important things

00:10:27.260 --> 00:10:29.380
about language and how we're going to use it

00:10:29.380 --> 00:10:33.120
and the only thing that the press are going
to care about in 2017

00:10:33.120 --> 00:10:35.420
is whether there is going to be a condom emoji.

00:10:36.660 --> 00:10:38.540
My name's Tom Scott, enjoy the night!

00:10:44.820 --> 00:10:46.980
MATT PARKER: Tom Scott, ladies and gentlemen!

00:10:54.100 --> 00:10:56.620
If we've learned anything from Tom Scott,

00:10:56.620 --> 00:10:59.240
it is: shtick happens.

00:10:59.240 --> 00:11:01.660
And vote yes on Proposition TR52!

00:11:02.900 --> 00:11:06.280
So there you go. And -- Tom! True YouTube
professional.

00:11:06.280 --> 00:11:09.520
He was talking, someone bumped a glass over,

00:11:09.520 --> 00:11:11.080
he paused for an edit break...

00:11:14.740 --> 00:11:16.540
TOM SCOTT: I didn't even notice I'd done that!

00:11:16.540 --> 00:11:19.800
MATT PARKER: and then picked up from the most
recent sentence fragment,

00:11:19.800 --> 00:11:22.220
so if you could all just mentally edit that
bit out...!

00:11:22.220 --> 00:11:24.220
[Translating these subtitles? Add your name here!]

